      </math>
    </region>
    <region left="0" top="738" width="432" height="98" color="#000000" fontSize="10">
      <text lang="eng" width="432" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
        <content>
          <p style="font-style: italic;">
            <span style="font-weight: bold;">Simulation results:</span>
            <br />vds_on: V(d) =0.117455715582 at 5e-06<br />id_on: abs(I(R12)) =0.0988254440605 at 5e-06<br />rds_on: VDS_on/ID_on=1.18851695227<br />p_on: 0.5*VDS_on*ID_on=0.00580380662491<br />delta_tj: P_on*310=1.79918005372</p>
        </content>
      </text>
    </region>
    <region left="0" top="1008" width="761" height="38" color="#000000" bgColor="#ffff00" fontSize="10">
      <text lang="eng" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
        <content>
          <p style="font-weight: normal; background-color: #ffff00;">Considering the circuit as shown above and Application Note AN-1001 (section 5.1), measure (in a simulator) td(on), td(off), tr, and tf of the transistor. Do the measured values match the datasheet? What do you think is the reason for any differences?</p>
        </content>
      </text>
    </region>
    <region left="0" top="1044" width="441" height="444" color="#000000">
      <picture>
        <raw format="png" encoding="base64"></raw>
      </picture>
    </region>
    <region left="0" top="1485" width="478" height="83" color="#000000" fontSize="10">
      <text lang="eng" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
        <content>
          <p>
            <span style="font-weight: bold;">Simulation results:</span>
            <br />td_on=7.37073677501e-09 FROM 2.36568361679e-09 TO 9.7364203918e-09<br />tr=5.1889222348e-09 FROM 9.7364203918e-09 TO 1.49253426266e-08<br />td_off=1.18515528854e-08 FROM 1.0022476336e-05 TO 1.00343278889e-05<br />tf=3.9773862807e-09 FROM 1.00343278889e-05 TO 1.00383052752e-05</p>
        </content>
      </text>
    </region>
    <region left="0" top="1575" width="645" height="23" color="#000000" fontSize="10">
      <text lang="eng" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
        <content>
          <p style="font-weight: bold;">Do the measured values match the datasheet? What do you think is the reason for any differences?</p>
        </content>
      </text>
    </region>
    <region left="0" top="1593" width="777" height="83" color="#000000" fontSize="10">
      <text lang="eng" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
        <content>
          <p style="font-weight: normal;">The simulated rise time was close to the typical datasheet value, while the turn-on delay was somewhat longer. Both the turn-off delay and the fall time were substantially shorter than the datasheet values. The test conditions likely contributed to these differences. Our circuit drove the gate to 3.3 V, compared with 10 V in the datasheet test, so less gate charge had to be removed before began to rise. This helps explain the shorter turn-off delay. The drain supply voltage, load resistance, and external gate resistance also differed and can affect the fall time.</p>
        </content>
      </text>
    </region>
    <region left="0" top="2007" width="769" height="53" color="#000000" bgColor="#ffff00" fontSize="10">
      <text lang="eng" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
        <content>
          <p style="font-weight: normal; background-color: #ffff00;">Change the value of R11 to 1kOhm. Considering Application Note AN-1001 (page 14 of the PDF) and the gate charging operations, can you identify the plateau (Vp in the application note)? What causes this plateau? What happens to the gate input current? How can you reduce this plateau?</p>
        </content>
      </text>
    </region>
    <region left="0" top="2070" width="594" height="297" color="#000000">
      <picture>
        <raw format="png" encoding="base64"></raw>
      </picture>
    </region>
    <region left="0" top="2367" width="759" height="164" color="#000000">
      <picture>
        <raw format="png" encoding="base64"></raw>
      </picture>
    </region>
    <region left="0" top="2529" width="755" height="173" color="#000000" fontSize="10">
      <text lang="eng" width="755" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
        <content>
          <p style="font-weight: bold;">What causes this plateau?<br /><span style="font-weight: normal;">The plateau is caused by the Miller effect. As VDS falls, much of the gate current goes into changing the charge on the gate–drain capacitance. As a result, VGS rises only slightly during this interval.</span><br /><br /><span style="font-weight: bold;">What happens to the gate input current?</span><br /><span style="font-weight: normal;">The gate current rises rapidly at turn-on, then stays at roughly 1.8mA in magnitude during the plateau. After the plateau, it approaches zero as VGS approaches 3.3V.</span><br /><br />How can you reduce this plateau?<br /><span style="font-weight: normal;">Reducing R11 from 1kOhm to the original 10Ohm allows more current to flow into the gate. This transfers the gate–drain charge more quickly and shortens the plateau.</span></p>
        </content>
      </text>
    </region>
    <region left="0" top="2718" width="776" height="23" color="#000000" bgColor="#ffff00" fontSize="10">
      <text lang="eng" width="776" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
        <content>
          <p style="background-color: #ffff00;">Can the turn-on and turn-off times of the transistor be reduced?</p>
        </content>
      </text>
    </region>
    <region left="0" top="2736" width="733" height="38" color="#000000" fontSize="10">
      <text lang="eng" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
        <content>
          <p>Yes. Both the turn-on and turn-off times can be reduced by charging and discharging the gate more quickly. In this circuit, reducing R11 from 1kOhm to 10Ohm increased the gate current during both transitions and shortens the Miller plateau.</p>
        </content>
      </text>
    </region>
    <region left="0" top="2772" width="772" height="166" color="#000000">
      <picture>
        <raw format="png" encoding="base64"></raw>
      </picture>
    </region>
